\section{Make Binary Interoperation an Explicit Contract}

The ISA may make it possible to pass an argument in any general-purpose register; two separately compiled functions can interoperate only if they select the same one. The ABI fixes shared choices about argument locations, return locations, register preservation, stack alignment, and other details that no instruction enforces by itself. The February 2024 VADL preprint, arXiv:2402.09087v1, describes a work-in-progress ABI section in Section~3.5; Sections~3.7 and~4.5.4 explain additional information used at the assembly and link boundaries \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}.

These are normalized VADL excerpts from Listings~19--20. They assume the relevant register file and surrounding ABI declarations:

\begin{verbatim}
alias register ra = X(1)
alias register sp = X(2)
alias register a0 = X(10)
return address = ra
[alignment: Bits<128>]
stack pointer = sp
\end{verbatim}

Here the alignment is 128 bits, or 16 bytes on a byte-addressed machine. An alias gives a name to an existing cell; it does not allocate another register. Section~3.5 also describes \texttt{caller saved} and \texttt{callee saved} groups and the \texttt{[preferred alias]} annotation. The compiler must treat a live caller-saved value differently across a call from a live callee-saved value; choosing a preferred name alone does not change either rule.

The preservation rule is an interface promise at the call boundary: a caller that needs a caller-saved value afterwards must preserve it itself, whereas a callee that changes a callee-saved value must restore it before returning. Stack alignment is likewise a property at each externally visible call point, not merely at function entry. A prologue may temporarily adjust the stack, but any nested call and any ABI-required aligned access must satisfy the applicable contract.

\section{Describe the Sequences That Carry ABI Meaning}

The \texttt{call sequence} example in Listing~21 uses the following VADL, with whitespace normalized. This is the paper's sample for an absolute address, not a claim that all RISC-V calls use this sequence.

\begin{verbatim}
call sequence (symbol: Address) = {
  LUI { rd = 1, imm20 = hi20(symbol) }
  JALR { rd = 1, rs1 = 1, imm = lo12(symbol) }
}
\end{verbatim}

The first instruction prepares the high part in register 1. The second consumes that value to form the target and writes the return address to the same register. The compiler can infer some simple operational patterns, but deciding that a particular sequence implements the agreed call convention requires ABI information. A position-independent call or a call via a stub would add further agreements about address computation and symbol resolution. The address-sequence discussion in Section~3.5 explicitly limits the presented address-load facility to absolute addresses; that statement does not imply that the paper's relocation system lacks relative relocations.

