\section{Resolve a Symbol Without Changing the Call's Meaning}

In this teaching trace, assume the target symbol eventually resolves to \texttt{0x12f90} and both instructions have four bytes. Abstractly, a relocation connects a patch site, a transformation such as ``high'' or ``low,'' and the symbol (with any applicable addend). This is an explanation of the contract, not a specification of ELF relocation IDs or storage fields.

\begin{verbatim}
emit LUI and JALR plus unresolved fixups for "helper"
layout: S = 0x12f90, A = 0
low bits = 0xf90 -> signed -112    # JALR extends the low part
high     = 0x13                   # round up to compensate
patch LUI with 0x13, JALR with 0xf90

execute:
  LUI  -> X(1) = 0x13000
  JALR -> target = 0x13000 - 112 = 0x12f90
        -> X(1) = address of instruction after JALR
\end{verbatim}

The JALR computation must read its base before writing \texttt{rd}; here both happen to name register~1. If an implementation writes the link first and then reads the base, it calls through the return address instead. If the high part is independently truncated to \texttt{0x12}, the assembled bits still look like two valid instructions but the call lands one page too low. Test a symbol just below and at the signed low-part boundary as well as the negative case. \Cref{fig:visual-328} shows where symbol identity must survive even when placeholder bytes contain no useful target.

