\section{Keep the File Format and Target Policy Apart}

ELF supplies general machinery for sections, symbols, and relocation records; it does not tell the linker how to patch a particular processor's split immediate. Section~4.5.4 describes reusing LLVM's object infrastructure while generating architecture-specific relocation declarations and behavior, including generic relocations for immediate fields. A PC-relative fixup may need the patch location in addition to the symbol. Even if both absolute and relative values fit the same bit layout, swapping their relocation meanings changes the program.

For a relative fixup, vary the distance between the section containing the patch and the section defining the symbol in a regression test. Moving both sections by the same amount cannot expose some address-base errors. Also check the offset at which the relocation is applied; patching a neighboring instruction with a legal immediate can leave the file readable while changing the wrong operation.

When changing an ABI, rebuild both sides of a call, and test new code against an independently built callee that checks incoming arguments, stack alignment, and preserved registers. A change to object conventions also needs tests on unresolved relocations and final linked bytes. For a proposed relaxation, check that the shorter form still reaches the symbol and preserves ABI-visible effects, including where the caller resumes. Section~5.5 exercises the prototype on RISC-V test executables. It does not establish arbitrary compatibility with external objects or validate all possible ABI optimizations.
